              "Your doctrine is the very first pick of the draft, and it lasts the whole draft. It decides how much of the opponent's roster you watch — and it pays you for looking away: the less you see, the larger your augment budget. There is no fourth option and you cannot change it later.",
          doctrinesTableCaption: "Doctrines: reveal versus augment points",
          doctrinesColumns: ["Doctrine", "What you see", "Augment points"],
          doctrines: [
              {
                  name: "Spymaster",
                  sees: "Every one of the opponent's creature picks, as they happen. Artifacts stay hidden.",
                  points: 5,
                  note: "Full information, smallest army budget.",
              },
              {
                  name: "Scout",
                  sees: "One random level-1 creature slot, one level-2 slot, and one of the two senior slots (level 3 or 4).",
                  points: 6,
                  note: "A partial read for a middling budget.",
              },
              {
                  name: "Battle Trance",
                  sees: "No slots are watched in advance. Colliding with a hidden opponent pick still reveals that specific slot.",
                  points: 7,
                  note: "Draft blind, field the strongest army.",
              },
          ],
          doctrinesOutro:
              "Those points are the whole augment budget you spend before placement, so doctrine is an army-size decision as much as an information one. If you select a hidden creature already taken by the opponent, the server reveals the collision and sends you back; a duplicate in an already watched slot is unavailable up front.",
          synergiesTitle: "Faction synergies: 2, 4, and 6 units",
          synergiesIntro:
              "Drafting 2/4/6 distinct roster units from one faction gives that faction synergy level 1/2/3 (3 units still give level 1, 5 give level 2); splitting one unit into several stacks does not raise the count. Each faction has a pair of possible bonuses, and the match ID deterministically selects one from every pair. Both sides get the same variant set, visible from the first draft screen. A synergy powers every unit in your army, whatever its faction. Its level is locked when the fight starts, so losses never lower it; a creature summoned mid-fight that is new to your army can raise it.",
          synergyLevels: "Power by level",
          synergies: [
              {
                  faction: "Life",
                  icon: "/assets/images/units/factions/life_128.webp",
                  options: [
                      "Supply: every stack grows by 6% / 12% / 19% when the fight starts, rounded down per stack",
                      "Morale & luck: +6/+2, +13/+5, +20/+9 for every unit",
                  ],
              },
              {
                  faction: "Chaos",
                  icon: "/assets/images/units/factions/chaos_128.webp",
                  options: [
                      "Movement: +1 / +2 / +3 steps for every unit",
                      "Break on attack: every damaging hit has a 6% / 11% / 17% chance to Break the target for 2 laps (abilities, auras and spells off)",
                  ],
              },
              {
                  faction: "Might",
                  icon: "/assets/images/units/factions/might_128.webp",
                  options: [
                      "Aura range: +1 / +2 / +3 cells for every aura in your army",
                      "Abilities power: +5 / +8 / +12 points to every unit's ability chances and strength, like extra luck",
                  ],
              },
              {
                  faction: "Nature",
                  icon: "/assets/images/units/factions/nature_128.webp",
                  options: ["Board units: +2 / +3 / +4 fielded stacks", `Flying armor: ${flyingArmorTiers} base armor for every flyer`],
              },
          ],
          placementTitle: "Placement: many fights are won before the first hit",
          placementParagraphs: [
              "Choose a roster unit, set its stack amount, and place it in your deployment zone. A drafted unit may be split into several stacks without creating extra bodies; you can then move, rebalance, or remove those stacks before locking placement.",
              "Footprints are unit-specific: ordinary pieces use 1x1, while large creatures can use 2x1 or 2x2. Larger footprints need more legal cells for routing and for escaping a narrowing edge.",
              "Before pressing start, check three things: who is inside your auras, who can be reached on the first turn, and where your ranged units will stand if the enemy dives them.",
          ],
          turnsTitle: "Turns: one action per turn",
          turnActions: [
              {
                  title: "Move",
                  text: "The path must be legal and fit inside the unit's Steps, and arriving ends the turn. Ending closer to the centre of the enemy army gives +3 morale and ending farther away −3; only a move that ends exactly as far away is neutral, so a sideways step usually costs 3.",
              },
              {
                  title: "Melee attack",
                  text: "The unit must already be adjacent or have a legal path to an adjacent attack cell within its Steps; the engine then moves it in and strikes, and the walk-in counts as a move for morale. A ranged unit without Handyman deals half damage in melee.",
              },
              {
                  title: "Ranged attack",
                  text: "You need shots, no enemy stack in any cell touching yours (diagonals count), and no Range Null Field or Rangebane. The arrow flies to a visible edge of the target: your own stacks never block it, the first enemy stack on the line takes it, and structures stop it. The built-in Sniper ability removes falloff entirely; the team Sniper augment only raises attack and widens each distance band, so its range divisor can still reach x8.",
              },
              {
                  title: "Spellbook",
                  text: "Spellbook units cast buffs, debuffs, healing, mass effects, summons, and position tricks. Magic resist cuts magic damage and spell-debuff chance; status resist lowers Stun/Paralysis chance and physical area damage; mind resist lowers the chance of mind effects.",
              },
              {
                  title: "Hourglass",
                  text: "The stack gives up its place and acts again at the very end of the lap, after the Dismorale stacks; several waiters keep their order. It costs 3 morale, works once per lap, is not available to your last stack still to act, and nobody can wait while a stack with Time Denial is alive.",
              },
              {
                  title: "Luck Shield / Skip",
                  text: "Luck Shield replaces this lap's random luck modifier with +3 (capped at +10), costs 2 morale, and ends the turn. Ending without acting or being forced to skip costs 1 morale; ending after moving or another action has no morale penalty.",
              },
          ],
          combatTitle: "Combat, without the formula sheet",
          combatParagraphs: [
              "Damage depends on attack, living bodies in the stack, target armor, luck, range distance, selected attack type, and active abilities. That is why the same unit can hit very differently from two different board states.",
              "A stack answers an attack once per lap (One in the Field: every attack), and the answer is spent even if it misses. The retaliation is computed from the stacks as they stand and lands before your blow, so a stack you are about to destroy still strikes back at full strength. A melee blow goes unanswered when the defender is Stunned, Blinded or Frozen, has No Melee, is Aggr-locked onto another stack, is barred from the attacker by Terrifying Gaze, or is under Cowardice facing a stack with more total health; Shadow Touch and Lightning Spin attacks are never answered.",
              "A shooter answers a shot only when it could shoot right now (arrows left, no enemy touching it, no Range Null Field or Rangebane); its counter-shot flies back along the line, hits the first enemy stack on it and costs an arrow. Splash from Area Throw, Large Caliber and Chakram lands before the counter-shot. Spells, Through Shot volleys and an Area Throw aimed at an empty cell are never answered.",
              "Turn order is fixed at the start of each lap. Stacks that rolled Morale act first; then the armies alternate one stack at a time, each sending its highest-initiative stack that has not acted (equal initiative is ordered at random), and when one army runs out the other's stacks go back to back; then the Dismorale stacks; then stacks waiting on the Hourglass, in the order they waited. In the first lap the army with the higher average morale starts (then the higher top initiative, then a coin flip).",
              "Abilities give fights their character: Lucky Strike can spike damage, Stun and Blindness break tempo, Petrifying Gaze can finish a target, Fire Shield returns damage, and Range Null Field shuts down shooting around it.",
          ],
          moraleTitle: "Morale, luck, and stack power",
          moraleItems: [
              "Morale is capped at −20…+20. At the start of each lap, the engine rolls a chance equal to its absolute morale. A positive roll (Morale) moves the stack ahead of the regular turn order with 1.25× damage for the lap; a negative roll (Dismorale) moves it behind every regular stack with 0.8× damage. Either way the stack keeps its one turn, and its morale cannot change until the lap ends. Morale does not grant an extra action.",
              "Every creature starts with its faction's morale: Life +4, Might +2, Nature +1, Chaos −1. Build more by moving toward the enemy army's geometric centroid (+3 per move) and destroying enemy stacks (+4 to whichever stack destroys it: attacker, retaliating defender or caster). Moving away costs 3, and losing a stack costs 4 morale for your other surviving stacks of that same unit. Deaths to Armageddon, narrowing, poison or Fire Wall change nobody's morale.",
              "Luck is capped at −10…+10 and changes incoming attack damage by about 1% per point; negative luck increases it. Spells, Armageddon, poison and Fire Wall ignore it. Each point also adds 1 percentage point to the stack's own ability chances and strength. Luck is re-rolled every lap: the faction base (Nature +4, Life and Might +1, Chaos −1) plus a fresh −3…+3 plus bonuses, so it never accumulates; Luck Shield replaces the roll with +3 for the rest of that lap.",
              "Stack power compares a stack's experience × living bodies with the strongest such value on the board, either army: up to 20% gives 1, up to 40% gives 2, up to 60% gives 3, up to 80% gives 4, above that 5. It is recalculated after every action and scales stack-powered abilities and auras, Limited Supply ammo, Heavy Armor and Chakram's target count; some spells need a minimum caster stack power. Every drafted stack is worth 1,000 experience, so an unsplit stack starts the fight at 5; split one in half and both halves drop to 3, and losses lower it further.",
              "In a stalled fight morale turns into movement: every lap in which the map narrows, or no stack ends closer to its nearest enemy, adds 0.05 steps per point of morale for the rest of the fight (+1 step at +20 after one such lap), and negative morale takes steps away.",
          ],
          moraleBreakdownTitle: "Exact morale changes",
          moraleBreakdown: [
              { change: "+3", text: "Move closer to the enemy army's centroid" },
              { change: "+4", text: "Destroy an enemy stack (goes to the stack that destroys it)" },
              { change: "-3", text: "Move farther from the enemy army's centroid" },
              { change: "-4", text: "Each surviving stack of yours of a unit whose stack was wiped out" },
              { change: "-3", text: "Hourglass (Wait) to act later in the queue" },
              { change: "-2", text: "Defend (Luck Shield)" },
              { change: "-1", text: "An actual skipped turn, including a timeout passed to the engine as a skip" },
              { change: "±20", text: "Hard cap — morale never leaves this range" },
          ],
          moraleBreakdownNote:
              "Ending after moving or another action costs nothing. At lap start, the roll chance is exactly the absolute morale percentage: positive acts first with 1.25× damage for the lap; negative acts late with 0.8× damage but does not lose its turn. The same ×1.25 / ×0.8 applies to retaliation, damage spells, Heal and Resurrection.",
          mapTitle: "The map does not wait forever",
          mapParagraphs: [
              "The board is a 16x16 grid. Ranked rolls one of three maps the moment the draft is created—each equally likely, one chance in three—and reveals it to both players together immediately after the Level-2 picks, before Level 3. The fight is played on exactly that map; it is never rerolled.",
          ],
          mapsTableCaption: "The three ranked maps",
          mapsColumns: ["Map", "Center", "Narrows", "Chance"],
          maps: [
              {
                  name: "Normal",
                  center: "Open stone field — no central hazard.",
                  narrows: "laps 4, 7 and 10",
                  chance: "1 in 3",
              },
              {
                  name: "FIRE PIT",
                  center: "A lava pool scars the center of the board until it dries at lap 10.",
                  narrows: "laps 4, 7 and 10",
                  chance: "1 in 3",
              },
              {
                  name: "Barrels",
                  center: "Exactly 12 destructible barrels, placed from the match ID across the four central columns; there is no single central block.",
                  narrows: "laps 5 and 9",
                  chance: "1 in 3",
              },
          ],
          mapsOutro:
              "A fourth map, Water, exists in the engine but is disabled in ranked, so you will never be dealt it. Narrowing turns the outer ring of cells into holes at the start of a lap and pushes units inward — sliding them to the nearest free spot — or destroys them when there is no room. If no stack of either army ends a lap closer to its nearest enemy, the next ring comes one lap early; if no damage was dealt that lap either, one extra ring is added for the rest of the fight. Nothing narrows after lap 12, and there are never more than 5 rings.",
          mapMechanicsTitle: "Two map rules worth knowing before you draft",
          mapMechanics: [
              {
                  title: "Lava is not walkable by default",
                  text: "Walking units cannot cross or finish on central lava and flyers can only fly over it; an innate Made of Fire ability (Efreet) allows both, and the Tier-2 Lava Striders artifact grants that access to the whole army. A lava-capable stack whose move crosses or ends on lava gains Made of Fire for 2 laps: +10% health, attack, armor, movement, initiative, shot range, magic resistance and ability power. It is not refreshed while active, and the pool dries into normal ground at the start of lap 10.",
              },
              {
                  title: "Shooting across Barrels",
                  text: "Barrels are structures, and an ordinary ranged attack is stopped by the first one in its lane. Double Shot spends up to two of its projectiles clearing barrels instead of hitting the target. Cyclops (Large Caliber) and Gargantuan (Area Throw) ignore structures outright — their shots fly over and destroy the barrels caught in the blast, which is what makes them the answer to a blocked lane.",
              },
          ],
          mapArmageddon:
              "Armageddon hits every stack of both armies at the start of laps 12, 13, 14 and 15. Armor, luck and magic resistance do not reduce it (a Water Shield absorbs one wave). Counting every body a stack started with, alive or dead, wave 1 deals 25% of their health (at least 75) and waves 2, 3 and 4 deal 50%, 75% and 100%. The first three add up to 150%, so a stack that faces all three dies to wave 3 unless Resurrection returned bodies or a Water Shield took a wave, and wave 4 destroys whatever is left. Armies that lose their last stacks to the same wave draw.",
          examplesTitle: "Role cheat sheet",
          examples: [
              "Black Dragon and Angel show what large flying finishers can do, but both need room for a 2x2 body.",
              "Medusa, Arbalester, and Gargantuan teach firing lanes, ammo, and range distance.",
              "Satyr and Ogre Mage are reminders that a spellbook can matter more than raw damage.",
              "Pegasus, Griffin, Peasant, and Valkyrie prove that aura placement wins fights before the first attack lands.",
          ],
          finalTitle: "How to play better next match",
          finalItems: [
              "Start with a faction plan: which 2/4/6 units unlock the synergy you actually want?",
              "Place ranged units so the enemy's first dive does not shut off their shooting.",
              "Do not press Hourglass automatically. Wait only when you know what better turn you are creating.",
              "Track the narrowing map from lap one, not when the edge is already a hole.",
          ],
      };

